From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:21:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 22/24] layout: lay out and paint <foreignObject> content

The inverse hinge, open. HTML inside a natively rendered SVG now goes
through real CSS layout and paints through the display list, above the
SVG image.

The seam already existed: a replaced box may carry an inner
IndependentFormattingContext -- the mechanism <video controls> uses for
its widget. Each <foreignObject> becomes one, built over its children
with BlockFormattingContext::construct, laid out against its declared
width and height, and wrapped in an anonymous PositioningFragment placed
at the rectangle the SVG transform chain maps it to.

Three separate defects stood between "fragments exist" and "pixels
appear", and each was found by testing rather than reading:

1. The foreign content was only reached when the SVG scene also
   produced an image. An SVG whose only child is a <foreignObject> has
   an empty scene and returned no fragments at all -- which is exactly
   the shape of document Mermaid builds when it measures labels. The
   scene image and the foreign content are now independent.

2. traverse_replaced_content, the paint traversal for a replaced box's
   children, matched LayoutRoot, Image, IFrame and Box, and dropped
   everything else in a catch-all -- including the PositioningFragment
   wrapper. The fragment tree was correct the whole time
   (getBoundingClientRect reported real rects); painting silently
   discarded it. A Positioning arm now pushes the fragment's offset and
   recurses.

3. position:absolute inside a <foreignObject> hoists into a positioning
   context, and a hoisted box that nobody lays out leaves a placeholder
   whose empty fragment slot panics the stacking-context builder --
   "Found hoisted box with missing fragment", crashing four WPT tests.
   Per SVG 2 the foreignObject establishes the containing block for such
   descendants, so the hoisted boxes are laid out against the foreign
   object's own rectangle, which is both the fix and the spec behavior.

Documented limitations, all visible rather than silent: foreign content
paints above the SVG image, so SVG drawn after a <foreignObject> still
paints beneath it (blending-svg-foreign-object and two paint-order
tests fail honestly where they previously passed vacuously -- neither
side used to render anything); and only the translation and scale of the
transform chain place the content, so a rotated <foreignObject> is
positioned correctly but not turned.

Validated: divs inside foreignObjects render with correct text at
correct positions, including through a translated <g>;
getBoundingClientRect on the inner content reports real geometry
([20,20,200,32] where it was [0,0]); foreign-object-margin-collapsing
and containing-block flip to passing; and Mermaid -- whose label
measurement depends on exactly this -- goes from three unlabelled
60x30 boxes to a correctly laid out flowchart. svg/ WPT +360 subtests
against the pref-off baseline, no crashes.
